finding: The delayed unforked rebuild leaves continuous collision detection with stale tree roots. physics_world.rs:235-239 snapshots dynamic/kinematic roots into continuous::ROOTS during solve_build_in_world (phase 5, call at 724), before the last solve worker rebuilds at solve.rs:785-787 (or the phase-6 fallback at physics_world.rs:746-747). treework.rs:170-177 publishes the potentially different root after tree::rebuild frees enlarged internal nodes and rebuilds from the free list (tree.rs:1040-1069, 926). No later call refreshes ROOTS; continuous.rs:350-363 subsequently traverses the cached roots for bullets, so a formerly-root node can now be only a subtree. This violates the review's root-read ordering and can miss bullet collisions even though hashes for the non-bullet corpus pass. The new unforked test only steps freshly created spheres, not enlarged trees with bullets. Also phase-6's debug_assert immediately after its fallback cannot detect an omitted solve-worker rebuild: both normal-dt tests would still pass that assertion via the fallback, so checked-green does not prove their named overlap path. No tracked edits or reruns were needed for this source-proven defect.
